# Organic Tetrabutylammonium Cation Intercalation to Heal Inorganic CsPbI3 Perovskite

## abstract
The in situ formation of reduced dimensional perovskite layer via post-synthesis ion exchange has been an effective way of passivating organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites. In contrast, cesium ions in Cs-based inorganic perovskite with strong ionic binding energy cannot exchange with those well-known organic cations to form reduced dimensional perovskite. Herein, we demonstrate that tetrabutylammonium (TBA+ ) cation can intercalate into CsPbI3 to effectively substitute the Cs cation and to form one-dimensional (1D) TBAPbI3 layer in the post-synthesis TBAI treatment. Such TBA cation intercalation leads to in situ formation of TBAPbI3 protective layer to heal defects at the surface of inorganic CsPbI3 perovskite. The TBAPbI3 -CsPbI3 perovskite exhibited enhanced stability and lower defect density, and the corresponding perovskite solar cell devices achieved an improved efficiency up to 18.32 % compared to 15.85 % of the control one.

## Organic Tetrabutylammonium Cation Intercalation to Heal Inorganic $\mathbf{Cs}\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{I}_{3}$ Perovskite
Though the last decade has witnessed significant progress in perovskite solar cells (PSCs),[1–4] the stability of PSCs owing to the release of organic cation, especially $\mathbf{MA}^{+}$ , from crystal lattice remains a major obstacle to its commercial application.[5–8] Compared to the organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites containing intrinsically instable organic cations, inorganic perovskites without volatile organic components in crystal lattice are one of the most promising candidates as highly stable photovoltaic (PV) materials.[9–14] Over the past years, inorganic perovskite with strong competence in energy conversion and great potential for applications in tandem devices has achieved tremendous progress as light absorber used in PSCs.[15–17] Several strategies have been developed to enhance their stability and performance, such as preparation of phases with low symmetry,[18,19] reduction of grain size,[20,21] alloying,[22,23] and so on.[24, 25]
Nevertheless, the Cs-based inorganic perovskites still face relatively poor PV performance than the hybrid perovskites due to defects. Post treatment using organic cation halide salt has been an effective strategy for healing the defects for hybrid perovskite.[26,27] Unlike the well-known organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites, ours and other previous studies have suggested that such post-treatment using conventional organic cation halide salt such as phenethylammonium iodide (PEAI) would not induce the formation of lowdimensional perovskite.[16,28–30] This is because that the binding strength of cesium ion in inorganic perovskites is too strong to be extracted from crystal lattice, which leads to surface organic cation termination rather than organic cation intercalation. So far, it is still a challenge to utilize the in situ formed low dimensional perovskite for passivating and stabilizing inorganic perovskite via regular facile post-synthesis treatment.
Here, we report for the first time an organic cation intercalation to heal $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ inorganic perovskite, in which a special quaternary ammonium salt, TBAI, was used as posttreatment agent. The $\mathrm{TBA^{+}}$ cation with strong intercalation ability functioned as low-dimension perovskite inducer, leading to the in situ formation of low-dimensional perovskite of 1D TBAPbI3. Such 1D perovskite intercalation helps to not only enhance the stability but also effectively passivate the defects of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ perovskite.[31]
The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ perovskite films were post-synthetically treated with different concentrations of TBAI isopropyl alcohol (IPA) solution $(0.5{-}2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ; Supporting Information, Figures S1 and S2). $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was found to be the optimal condition considering both stability and performance, and corresponding thin films hereinafter are referred to as TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ . Unlike previous organic cation termination reports with no new perovskite peaks, a new diffraction peak at about $7.2^{\circ}$ was observed in the TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ sample as shown in Figure 1a, which does not belong to the TBAI salt (Supporting Information, Figure S3), indicating the formation of new structure or compound. To confirm whether the peak is ascribed to a low-dimensional perovskite, $\mathrm{TBA^{+}}$ based single crystal was grown using precursor of TBAI and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (Supporting Information, Figure S4). A scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image (Supporting Information, Figure S5) showed clearly needle-like single crystal. Singlecrystal X-ray diffraction analysis (Figure 1 b) indicated that the TBAI- $\mathrm{\cdotPbI}_{2}$ precursor tends to form a 1D perovskite of $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ with space group $I\bar{4}2d$ (Supporting Information, Figure S6).[43] Both the grinded $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ perovskites powder XRD pattern and the simulated single crystal XRD in Figure 1b show a characteristic peak at $7.2^{\circ}$ ascribed to (200) plane, confirming the formation of $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ on the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ perovskite. High resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) image in Figure 1c further proved the existence of 1D $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite in the TBAI- $\mathrm{.csPbI}_{3}$ system. Moreover, grazing incidence wide angle $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray scattering (GIWAXS) analysis confirmed that the 1D $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ is highly oriented compared to the reference one (Supporting Information, Figure S7). As shown in Figure 1d, one strong diffraction spot at $\mathbf{q}_{z}=5.2\ \mathrm{nm}^{-1}$ was observed, which can be ascribed to (200) plane of $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ .
On the other hand, the ultraviolet visible (UV/Vis) spectra (Supporting Information, Figure S8) show the treatment had a negligible effect on the optical absorption, indicating that only a small amount of 1D perovskite were formed. The absorption edges of the films are located at about $735\mathrm{nm}$ (band gap ca. $1.68\mathrm{eV}$ ), which is in consistent with the previously reported black phase $\mathsf{\beta-C s P b I}_{3}$ perovskite.[19] $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS; Supporting Information, Figure S9) suggested that there is no apparent peak shifting between TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ , which could be ascribed to the small amount of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ converted into 1D $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite. Furthermore, it has been confirmed that the direct introduction of TBAI into the precursor solution can lead to a poor-quality film (Supporting Information, Figure S10), demonstrating that $\mathrm{\DeltaTBA^{+}}$ is too large to form mixed-cation perovskite with $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ . The ToF-SIMS results in the Supporting Information, Figure S11 suggest that the $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ layer is uniformly distributed on the surface of the film.
Such 1D layer significantly changed morphological properties of thin film. Figure 2a showed that $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film undergone TBAI treatment exhibits some grain regrowth. The grain boundaries become smoother and more compact, the cause of which can be excluded from IPA (Supporting Information, Figure S12). Cross-sectional SEM images (Figure 2b) also showed that thin film with TBAI treatment contained less pinholes inside. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ single crystal and thin film are quite stable (Supporting Information, Figure S13). With the $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ layer, the phase stability of thin films was also significantly enhanced (Supporting Information, Figure S14), probably because the formed 1D perovskite can work as terminal group as well.[32–34]
As previous works have proved that Cs-based halide perovskite cannot go through simple ion exchange reaction with the incoming organic cations to form a conventional 2D capping layer.[28,29] Here we propose an explanation and mechanism for the formation of 1D structure in this work. Generally, tetrabutylammonium cation based compounds like tetrabutylammonium hydroxide (TBAOH) is one well known exfoliating agent widely used in the delamination of layered perovskite oxides (Ruddlesden–Popper phases for example, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CaTa}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}$ , Dion–Jacobsen phases for example, $\mathrm{KCa_{2}N b_{3}C_{10}})$ .[35–38] The TBA cation can carry out ion exchange reaction with these A site inorganic cations in perovskites oxides.[37,39] This suggested that the TBA cation exhibits strong binding energy to intercalate into inorganic perovskite to replace inorganic cations. This is also confirmed by the simulated structural data of single crystal (Supporting Information, Figure S6) that $\mathrm{TBA^{+}}$ does appear inside two layers of $\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}$ octahedra. We propose that bulky $\mathrm{TBA^{+}}$ cations can intercalate into the $\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}$ octahedra and exchange interlayer $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ , as shown in Figure 2c. Subsequently, two closely connected layers fractured and the three-dimensional perovskite structure collapsed, while a one-dimensional perovskite formed ultimately. To verify whether the $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ can be fully exchanged by $\mathrm{TBA^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ thin film was soaked in TBAI-IPA solution. 3D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ diffraction peaks vanished after $12\mathrm{{h}}$ soaking and only strong $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ peaks were detected, showing a total conversion from 3D to 1D perovskite (Supporting Information, Figure S15). Inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis was also used to determine the $\mathrm{Cs}$ ion leaching in the final solution. According to the data in the Supporting Information, Table S1, the intercalation of $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ leads to the leaching of $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ into IPA solution, which is consistent with the observed conversion of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ into $\mathrm{TBAPbI}_{3}$ . We also exposed the fresh $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ thin films into the IPA solvent and PEAI-IPA solution to exclude the effect of IPA solvent on the ion exchange between IPA and TBA+, as shown in the Supporting Information, Figure S16.
The enhanced intensity with a slight circa $2\mathrm{nm}$ blue-shift of the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra in the Supporting Information, Figure S17 suggested that the nonradiative recombination was effectively reduced.[40] According to the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay curves in Figure 3a and the average charge carrier lifetime calculated from equation $\begin{array}{r l}{\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}=}&{{}\frac{\smile\bar{\Sigma}A_{i}\tau_{i}^{2}}{\Sigma A_{i}\tau_{i}}}\end{array}$ SAiti2, the value for TBAI$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ was twice as much as the control one (6.58 ns for TBAI- $\mathrm{.csPbI_{3}}$ , compared to $3.16~\mathrm{{ns}}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3,}$ ). The prolonged carrier lifetime agrees with the observation of suppressed non-radiative recombination.
Trap-state density $(N_{\mathrm{t}})$ was measured from trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ , obtained from space-charge-limited current (SCLC) test of electron-only devices. The measured $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ of TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ perovskite electron-only devices were $0.272{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $0.303{\mathrm{V}}$ respectively, as shown in Figure 3b. According to the following equation:
$$
V_{\mathrm{TFL}}=\frac{e L^{2}N_{\mathrm{t}}}{2\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}}\varepsilon},
$$
where $e$ is the elementary charge, $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite films, $\varepsilon$ is the relative permittivity of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ ( $\overset{\cdot}{\varepsilon}=$ 6.32) and $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity.[41] Therefore, the obtained trap state densities, $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ could be calculated as $6.74\times$ $10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $7.51\times10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively for the two perovskite systems. The increased charge-recombination lifetime and reduced charge-extraction time (Supporting Information, Figure S18) demonstrated that the trap-assisted carrier recombination was reduced. All results above stated clearly the effective passivation derived from 1D perovskite.
Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was used to determine the working function (WF) and the valence band energy $(E_{\mathrm{VB}})$ of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ and TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ thin films.[42] The WF of pristine perovskite, determined from the secondary electron onset, is $4.154\mathrm{eV},$ which shifted to $3.832\mathrm{eV}$ after the TBAI post-synthesis procedure (Figure 3 c). The calculated $E_{\mathrm{VB}}$ and corresponding conduction band energy $(E_{\mathrm{CB}})$ of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ and TBAI- $\mathrm{.csPbI}_{3}$ showed the latter was $0.05\mathrm{eV}$ lower than the former as illustrated in Figure $3\mathrm{d}$ , which improved energy-level alignment, hence promoting PV performance.
Typical planar PSCs with the configuration of $\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}/$ Perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag were assembled. The TBAI$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ based devices show better reproducibility and higher PV performance than $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ based devices (Supporting Information, Figure S19). The distinctly elevated photovoltaic performance mainly came from the enhancement of the open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and fill factor (FF), which is because of the synergistic effect of defect passivation and better energy-level alignment. Evidently, the champion TBAI$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ -based PSC exhibits an improved PCE of $18.32\%$ , while that of its pristine $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ -based counterpart is $15.85\%$ (Figure 4a). As shown in the Supporting Information, Figure S20, the stable output of a champion TBAI- $\mathrm{.csPbI}_{3}$ based device exhibits a stabilized PCE of $17.6\%$ at the maximum power point of $0.96{\mathrm{V}}.$ The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value $(20.28\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}},$ ) obtained from the $J{-}\mathbf{V}$ curve is well consistent with the integrated value $(19.48\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2},$ ) from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) curve in the Supporting Information, Figure S21.
Besides the enhance PV performance, TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ - based PSCs also exhibit better stability than $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ -based PSCs, as shown in Figure 4 b. Under 500 hours of continuous illumination at the maximum power point (MPP), the TBAI$\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ -based PSC without encapsulation maintained circa $17\%$ PCE, almost $93\%$ of its initial stage, whereas the PCE of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ -based PSC had a rapid drop and the device efficiency had a precipitous decay to about $10\%$ of its original value.
In summary, we reported a brand-new 1D layer healing strategy for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ perovskite via TBAI treatment. With the defects greatly reduced and energy-level alignment improved, the TBAI- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ thin films showed greater phase stability, and PSCs based on these films exhibited better PV performance. This work may provide future promising directions of 1D protected all-inorganic perovskites.
